@title Problem node: uncoupled pheromone becomes noise
@keywords problem-node, pheromone, trail, signal, entropy
@audience ant-system designers, library authors
@tone plain
@style pattern
@why committed pattern authority: library/ants/pheromone-trail-tuner.flexiarg, conclusion and HOWEVER, lines 25-37 (introduced at futon3 521967f)
@how no mechanism claimed at problem grain; the source pattern's THEN names the adaptive trail-tuning mechanism

! conclusion: This is a problem-stating node: without adaptive scaling, pheromone laying either floods the board with entropy or fails to leave useful breadcrumbs.

  + context: Minted by library-loop problem-authoring batch A4 from committed pattern history; it exists so pheromone-control mechanisms have a problem node to land on.

  + IF:
    Pheromone trails are intended to communicate useful discoveries and corridor state.

  + HOWEVER:
    The problem, verbatim from the committed source: “Without adaptive scaling, ants either flood the board with trails (entropy) or fail to leave breadcrumbs when scouts discover food deserts.” (source: futon3 library/ants/pheromone-trail-tuner.flexiarg:35-37; introduced by commit 521967f)

  + THEN:
    Treat this node as the landing point for @why edges from patterns that answer the pheromone signal/noise problem; solving it remains the mechanism pattern's work.

  + BECAUSE:
    A separate problem node records why trail regulation is needed without claiming the regulator itself.

